From c10c2f0bd69f5ce9a06826fa0da15f2be0139146 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:45:56 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 79/82] kosmickrisp: widen 16-bit varyings to the slot Metal
 carries

storageInputOutput16 was withheld with a note that
dEQP-VK.spirv_assembly.instruction.graphics.16bit_storage.* failed on a vertex
to fragment interface mismatch.

Varying compaction gives every varying a float type of its own size, to help
it vectorize the interface, so a 16-bit integer varying reaches the backend
typed float16 and is transported as the bits of a half. Metal's raster
interface does not carry every half bit pattern: the 16-bit integer cases fail
on the values whose bits are a half denormal or a NaN payload, while the 16-bit
float cases, which only ever carry numbers, pass. The declared width was the
second half of the problem, because the two stages are translated separately
and a slot's Metal type came from whichever access the gather saw last, so a
vertex shader writing four components and a fragment shader reading two
disagreed.

Widen a varying narrower than 32 bits to 32 bits before the chain is linked,
while the intrinsics still say whether the varying holds an integer or a float.
A 16-bit varying already occupies a whole 32-bit slot here, because this driver
does not let compaction pack two of them into one, so the widening costs no
varying space and puts 16-bit input and output on the path the 32-bit varyings
have always used - including the bit-exact transport an integer varying needs.
Widening a float converts rather than reinterprets, which is exact in both
directions and leaves interpolation to run at the higher precision Vulkan
allows.

The two interfaces Metal describes in their own widths are left alone: vertex
attributes, which Metal fetches in the vertex format's width, and fragment
outputs, which are render targets.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
